## Reading truncated messages

The roadmap is a compressed view of the session. Under budget pressure a
message is cut down to its assigned floor and marked with `… [+N chars]`,
naming the omitted char count. A message with no marker is shown in full.

When a `… [+N chars]` marker sits on a message whose full content would
change your judgment on a specific candidate finding — the `new_string`
of an Edit under review, the output of a test or build in a tool result,
the reasoning of a thinking block that decided the change — fetch that
message's full text before deciding. Query one line at a time by line
number:

`SELECT line, text FROM message WHERE session_id = '{{ session_id }}' AND line = 42`

Before dismissing a candidate finding as unfounded, if the evidence
would come from a truncated message, fetch that message first. The
roadmap's truncation is a signal to look, not a signal to conclude.

Query only the specific lines whose truncation blocks a decision. Do
not scan ranges, and do not fetch messages the roadmap already shows in
full. The roadmap covers every message of the session's reviewed types
(user prompts, assistant text and thinking, and both sides of tool
calls); system and meta messages are deliberately omitted and do not
carry code-review evidence worth chasing.

## Writing findings

Write findings using the `mcp__gage__Finding` tool using a single request
rather than one tool use per finding. This saves time and tokens.

If you did not find issues for the session, do not use `mcp__gage__Finding`
to report "no issues found". Only use the tool to report found issues.

In your reply, simply state whether you wrote findings. Do not re-state the
findings in your reply.

